---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { href, ROUTES } from "../lib/routes";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="About"
  description="What Knowledge Hub is for, and the editorial standard every lesson is held to."
>
  <main id="main-content" class="prose-page page-width" tabindex="-1">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>What this is for</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Knowledge Hub exists to make one difficult software concept concrete
      quickly. Not to cover everything, and not to replace the documentation.
    </p>

    <h2>The promise</h2>
    <p>
      A lesson should take three to five minutes. In that time you should be
      able to explain the mental model in your own words, recognize when the
      idea applies, and name a limitation that still matters. If a lesson does
      not do that, it is not finished.
    </p>

    <h2>How a lesson is built</h2>
    <p>
      Every lesson starts with an animation that demonstrates the idea from its
      opening state to a result, then repeats. The same underlying description
      produces the animation, the static step sequence, and the text a screen
      reader announces, so no reader gets a lesser version of the explanation.
    </p>
    <p>
      A lesson is data, not code. It is validated before it can publish: broken
      references, unreachable steps, missing accessibility content, and
      incomplete evidence all fail the build rather than reaching a reader.
    </p>

    <h2>The editorial standard</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every factual claim traces to a primary source, recorded with the date
        it was checked. A blog post is not sufficient support on its own.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every lesson names a real limitation. A technique that always works is a
        technique that has not been examined.
      </li>
      <li>
        Real-world applications describe actual software situations, with the
        production constraint that still bites. Toy restatements do not count.
      </li>
      <li>
        Code samples run in the test suite. A sample that is not executed does
        not ship.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts, no backend, and no analytics. The site remembers
      nothing about you: no progress, no bookmarks, no history. Every page is
      identical for everyone. That means it cannot offer to resume where you
      left off, which is a real cost and a deliberate trade for keeping the
      thing simple and private.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are also no scores, streaks, or leaderboards. Understanding is the
      point, and measuring it would change what the lessons optimize for.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading and contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Start with <a href={href(ROUTES.paths)}>Interview Foundations</a>, or
      <a href={href(ROUTES.explore)}>browse every lesson</a>. The source lives
      on GitHub; corrections to a lesson's facts or evidence are the most useful
      thing you can send.
    </p>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .prose-page {
    max-width: var(--max-width-prose);
    padding-block: var(--section-space);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--color-accent-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--letter-spacing-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-3xl);
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-block: var(--space-9) var(--space-5);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
  }

  p,
  li {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--line-height-relaxed, 1.7);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
  }

  ul {
    display: grid;
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-7);
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
